,{"className":"line","children":[["$","span",null,{"style":{"--shiki-light":"#032F62","--shiki-dark":"#9ECBFF"},"children":"  \"gpu_count\""}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"--shiki-light":"#24292E","--shiki-dark":"#E1E4E8"},"children":": "}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"--shiki-light":"#032F62","--shiki-dark":"#9ECBFF"},"children":"\"minimum\""}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"--shiki-light":"#24292E","--shiki-dark":"#E1E4E8"},"children":","}]]}],"\n",["$","span",null,{"className":"line","children":[["$","span",null,{"style":{"--shiki-light":"#032F62","--shiki-dark":"#9ECBFF"},"children":"  \"gpu_model\""}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"--shiki-light":"#24292E","--shiki-dark":"#E1E4E8"},"children":": "}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"--shiki-light":"#032F62","--shiki-dark":"#9ECBFF"},"children":"\"exact\""}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"--shiki-light":"#24292E","--shiki-dark":"#E1E4E8"},"children":","}]]}],"\n",["$","span",null,{"className":"line","children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"--shiki-light":"#24292E","--shiki-dark":"#E1E4E8"},"children":"}"}]}]]}]}]}]
1a:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"gpu_count"}]," as ",["$","code",null,{"children":"minimum"}]," so it is both a threshold and consumable: a worker\nwith four GPUs can hold four single-GPU actions. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"gpu_model"}]," as ",["$","code",null,{"children":"exact"}]," so an\naction needing a particular device does not land on a different one."]}]
1b:["$","div",null,{"className":"my-6 flex gap-3 rounded-xl border px-5 py-4 border-amber-500/40 bg-amber-50/60 text-foreground dark:bg-amber-500/10","role":"alert","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"20","height":"20","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"1.75","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400","aria-hidden":"true","children":"$12:props:children:0:props:children"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1 [&>p]:my-0 [&>p+p]:mt-2","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1 font-mono text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]","children":"One shipped example gets this wrong"}],["$","p",null,{"children":["The Kubernetes example config in ",["$","code",null,{"children":"kubernetes/forge/forge-config.json5"}],"\ndeclares both ",["$","code",null,{"children":"gpu_count"}]," and ",["$","code",null,{"children":"gpu_model"}]," as ",["$","code",null,{"children":"priority"}],", which, per the\nsection above, enforces nothing. An action requesting four GPUs will schedule\nonto a worker with none. If you started from that file, change both."]}]]}]]}]
1c:["$","p",null,{"children":["The same pattern covers everything else that varies. An ",["$","code",null,{"children":"os"}]," and an ",["$","code",null,{"children":"arch"}]," key\nas ",["$","code",null,{"children":"exact"}]," give you cross-platform pools from one scheduler. A ",["$","code",null,{"children":"license_seats"}],"\nkey as ",["$","code",null,{"children":"minimum"}]," makes a vendor tool's seat count a scheduling constraint rather\nthan a race, because ",["$","code",null,{"children":"minimum"}]," is consumable: the seats are held for the duration\nof the action and released when it finishes. That last one is a genuinely useful\ntrick for EDA flows and is not apparent from the config reference."]}]
1d:["$","$L6",null,{"as":"h2","id":"running-a-heterogeneous-fleet","children":"Running a heterogeneous fleet"}]
1e:["$","p",null,{"children":"The pool is not one shape of machine. It's several, each a worker process with\nits own configuration, all registering with the same scheduler."}]
1f:["$","$L3f",null,{"children":"flowchart TB\nS[\"scheduler<br/>declares which keys exist<br/>and how each is matched\"]\nS -->|\"cpu_count ≥ 4\"| C[\"compile pool<br/>many small workers\"]\nS -->|\"memory_kb ≥ 64000000\"| L[\"link pool<br/>few large-memory workers\"]\nS -->|\"gpu_count ≥ 1<br/>gpu_model = a100\"| G[\"GPU pool\"]\nS -->|\"os = windows\"| O[\"platform pool\"]"}]
20:["$","p",null,{"children":["Nothing about this requires more than one scheduler, or a separate deployment\nper pool. Each worker announces what it is; the scheduler routes on that.\n",["$","$L3c",null,{"href":"/how-to/run-multiple-workers","children":"Run multiple workers"}]," is the mechanics, and\n",["$","$L3c",null,{"href":"/operate/tuning","children":"tuning"}]," covers sizing each pool once you're running them."]}]
21:["$","p",null,{"children":"The practical advice is to start with one pool and split it only when you can\nname the reason. Two pools with different properties is a real operational cost,\nand it pays for itself when a specific class of action is either starved of the\nhardware it needs or hogging hardware it doesn't."}]
22:["$","$L6",null,{"as":"h2","id":"toolchains-are-the-hard-part-not-the-scheduling","children":"Toolchains are the hard part, not the scheduling"}]
23:["$","p",null,{"children":"For this class of build the routing is the straightforward half. The half that takes the\ntime is making the actions hermetic enough to run somewhere else at all."}]
24:["$","p",null,{"children":"Large old codebases accumulate assumptions: a compiler at an absolute path, a\ntool installed by the platform team years ago, an environment variable set in a\nshell profile, a vendor toolchain with a license daemon. All of that works\nlocally and none of it exists on a worker. Getting a large build onto remote\nexecution is mostly the work of finding those assumptions and declaring them."}]
25:["$","p",null,{"children":["Forge's answer is ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"Local Remote Execution"}],": building the toolchain\nitself as a declared, reproducible artifact so that the environment a worker\nruns in is the same one you get locally, by construction rather than by\nconvention. ",["$","$L3c",null,{"href":"/explanations/lre","children":"Local Remote Execution"}]," is the explanation, and\n",["$","$L3c",null,{"href":"/remote-execution/toolchains-and-hermeticity","children":"Toolchains and hermeticity"}]," is\nthe practical path."]}]
26:["$","p",null,{"children":"This is also the reason to be honest about sequencing. Getting started (caching) costs\nyou a URL in a config file. Remote execution costs you a toolchain project. The payoff on\na build of this size justifies it, but it is a project, and planning it as a\nconfig change is how these migrations stall."}]
27:["$","$L6",null,{"as":"h2","id":"what-a-build-like-this-looks-like-when-it-works","children":"What a build like this looks like when it works"}]
28:["$","p",null,{"children":["One published account is worth reading rather than paraphrasing:\n",["$","$L3c",null,{"href":"https://reidkleckner.dev/posts/llvm-recc-forge/","children":"Reid Kleckner's write-up"}],"\ndescribes building LLVM with ",["$","code",null,{"children":"recc"}]," executing remotely against Forge and\ntaking a build from roughly seventeen minutes to roughly four. It is one\nperson's measurement on their own hardware, with caveats the post itself spells\nout (precompiled headers disabled, a mixed home fleet, a wireless link), so\ntreat it as an illustration of the shape rather than a number to plan against,\nbut the shape is right. A wide graph of independent compiles is exactly the case\nwhere adding remote cores collapses the wall clock."]}]
29:["$","p",null,{"children":"The honest general statement is that speedups on this class of build vary by an\norder of magnitude between workloads, because they depend on how wide the graph\nis, how long the critical path is, and how much of the build was already\ncacheable. The way to find out is to measure your own."}]
2a:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","$L3c",null,{"href":"/getting-started/connect-your-build/cmake-recc","children":"CMake with recc"}]," is the path\nfor CMake-based C++ trees, which is what most large non-Bazel C++ projects are."]}]
2b:["$","$L6",null,{"as":"h2","id":"common-questions","children":"Common questions"}]
2c:["$","$L40",null,{"children":[["$","$L41",null,{"title":"My build is CMake or Make, not Bazel. Is any of this available?","id":"my-build-is-cmake-or-make-not-bazel-is-any-of-this-available","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Partly. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"recc"}]," puts a CMake-based build on the remote cache by intercepting\ncompiler invocations, and it's the documented path for large C++ trees. The\nsetup in these docs is cache-only (",["$","code",null,{"children":"RECC_CACHE_ONLY=1"}],", so misses compile\nlocally); recc can also forward compiles to remote workers once you have them,\none compiler invocation at a time. Remote execution of the whole graph needs a\nbuild tool that describes every action's complete input set, which is what\nBazel, Buck2, Siso and BuildStream do and what Make does not."]}]}],["$","$L41",null,{"title":"How do I stop huge link actions from crowding out compiles?","id":"how-do-i-stop-huge-link-actions-from-crowding-out-compiles","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Give them different properties and let ",["$","code",null,{"children":"minimum"}]," do the accounting. A link\naction declaring a large ",["$","code",null,{"children":"memory_kb"}]," only matches workers that have it, and\nbecause ",["$","code",null,{"children":"minimum"}]," is consumable, one such action occupies that worker's memory\nbudget until it finishes rather than letting three more land on top of it. If\nthey are still colliding, that's the signal to split the pool."]}]}],["$","$L41",null,{"title":"Can workers run different operating systems?","id":"can-workers-run-different-operating-systems","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Yes: a worker is a process, and pools are distinguished only by the properties\nthey declare. Declare ",["$","code",null,{"children":"os"}]," as ",["$","code",null,{"children":"exact"}]," on the scheduler, have each worker declare\nits own, and one scheduler routes to all of them. The constraint is not\nForge's; it's that the action's toolchain has to exist for the target\nplatform."]}]}],["$","$L41",null,{"title":"Does a vendor tool's license server work with this?","id":"does-a-vendor-tools-license-server-work-with-this","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["The tool still talks to the license server as it always did, so the worker\nneeds network access to it. What changes is that seat exhaustion becomes a\nscheduling problem you can express: a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"license_seats"}]," key matched as ",["$","code",null,{"children":"minimum"}],"\nholds seats for the duration of an action and releases them after, which turns\na race into a queue."]}]}],["$","$L41",null,{"title":"Is the cache still worth it if most of my actions are unique?","id":"is-the-cache-still-worth-it-if-most-of-my-actions-are-unique","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"Usually yes, because \"most actions are unique\" is rarely true across a whole\ntree: the third-party dependencies, the toolchain, and everything below the\nchange you made are shared. It stops being true for workloads that are\ngenuinely new every run, and some ML training and fuzzing jobs are exactly that.\nFor those, remote execution is the part that helps and the cache is close to\nfree anyway."}]}]]}]
